    "description": "The attacker-controlled 'next' parameter is filtered by is_local_path, which only checks the raw string prefix and then trusts urlsplit; urlsplit strips embedded ASCII tab/CR/LF, so '/\\t\\t//evil.com' (='///evil.com' after stripping) is seen as a local path with empty netloc and returned as the 302 Location. Browsers strip those same control characters but parse '///evil.com' as a scheme-relative URL (extra slashes are skipped in the whatwg 'special authority ignore slashes' state), so a crafted link such as ?action=logout&value=x&next=/\\t\\t//evil.com (or the login action, which redirects right after issuing a session) bounces victims to an attacker-controlled origin for phishing; the logout path requires no credentials at all."
